;QZA-19/0243;Blue Justice initiaive- NFD;QZA-0864;Hav øvrige tiltak;B;2025;28.10.2025;Actual;258 000;0;NOK;2020;2025;Nærings- og fiskeridepartementet;Grant;470301;Seksjon for klima, hav og næringsutvikling;0163;0163.71;0163.71.111;Bærekaftige hav og tiltak mot marin forsøpling;Global Unspecified;ANTH;Thomsen, Anja;;03;;26.06.2020;NFD - Nærings- og fiskeridepartementet;NFD - Nærings- og fiskeridepartementet;Norwegian public sector;V;Delegation to governmental grant manager;Bilateral;Other technical assistance;31310;313;Fishing;10;Fishing policy and administrative management;;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;The Blue Justice Initiative (BJI) supports the two Minister Declarations on Transnational Organized Crime in the Global Fishing Industry. Both Declarations recognise the inter-continental flow of illegal fish products, illicit money and human trafficking victims in transnational organized crime cases in the global fishing industry. The Declarations emphasise the need for all regions of the world to cooperate when investigating these criminal offences. The Blue Justice Initiative addresses the need to cooperate and build capacity to address transnational organized crime in the global fishing industry. The goal of BJI is to identify the measures needed to address transnational organised fisheries crime. Criminals seek out the most vulnerable regions of the world, typically those with limited resources to prevent and combat organised crime. An important part of the Blue Justice Initiative is therefore to support developing countries in building capacity. This agreement supports activities targeting developing countries and also salary and operating expenses for responsible staff in the Ministry of Trade, Industry and Fisheries related to these activities. The BJI is an initiative initiated by the Ministry of Trade, Industry and Fisheries.;;
