;QZA-23/0039;Supporting adaptation capacity through increased insurance penetration in Africa (SACPIP-Africa);QZA-1026;Tap og skade;B;2025;31.10.2025;Planned;1 208 721;0;;2024;2028;;;300516;KNN-NAKLI Seksjon for natur og klima;0163;0163.70;0163.70.131;Miljø og klima;Africa Regional;NORAD-LAGI;Gisnås, Lauren Céline Naville;2301627;03;;13.11.2024;African Risk Capacity Limited;African Risk Capacity Limited;NGO International;I;Project- and programme grant;Bilateral;Project-type interventions;43060;430;Other multisector;60;Disaster Risk Reduction;;-;-;-;-;-;Significant;Significant;-;-;-;-;-;Main;-;Main;-;The activities in this project aim at expanding parametric insurance coverage throughout the African continent by enhancing engagement with ARC member states and diversifying the ARC Ltd. risk pool. More specifically, this project will “increase the financial resilience of populations that are vulnerable to climate-related disasters by equipping states to forecast, assess and manage the impacts of natural disasters and ensuring that households receive timely support”. This project supports countries to adapt to climate change and address loss and damage. As per ARC's gender strategy, gender issues are taken into consideration throughout the systematic process of disaster risk mitigation and the ARC program life cycle. E.g. gender is built into the capacity building work and in the risk models and contingency planning processes. Gender-responsive actions are monitored and supported. ;Støtte til ARC Ltd. som en del av ARC Group. Finansiering til klimatilpasning og tap og skade. ;
;QZA-23/0040;Climate Resilience and Early Warning Systems (CREWS);QZA-0982;Climate services;B;2025;26.08.2025;Actual;25 000 000;0;NOK;2023;2025;IBRD;Grant;300516;KNN-NAKLI Seksjon for natur og klima;0163;0163.70;0163.70.131;Miljø og klima;Global Unspecified;NORAD-U15321;Lunde, Lars Andreas;2302057;03;;12.12.2023;IBRD - International Bank for Reconstruction and Development;WMO - World Meteorological Organisation;International Monetary Fund (IMF), World Bank (WB) and World Trade Organisation (WTO);I;Project- and programme grant;Earmarked to multilaterals;Contributions to multi-donor/multi-entity funding mechanisms;41010;410;General environmental protection;10;Environmental policy and administrative management;;-;-;-;-;-;Main;Significant;-;-;-;-;-;Main;-;Main;-;CREWS is a trust fund with The International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (the World Bank) as trustee. Decisions on allocations of funds within the CREWS Initiative are taken by a Steering Committee where all contributors to the CREWS Trust Fund are represented as decision making members. To support the work of the Steering Committee it is established a secretariat that is accountable to the Steering Committee for the performance of its functions. The CREWS Secretariat is hosted by WMO in Geneva under WMO management and applicable policies and procedures. As of today there are three implementing partners of the CREWS Initiative: World Bank/GFDRR

