;QZA-24/0049;Increasing the transparency and accountability of the UNSR;QZA-0268;UN-reform;B;2025;18.02.2025;Actual;2 800 000;0;NOK;2024;2026;SECURITY COUNCIL REPORT;Grant;300533;HUM-FOS Seksjon for forebygging og stabilisering;0151;0151.73;0151.73.111;FN og globale utfordringer;Global Unspecified;NORAD-U16715;Østnor, Kjetil;;03;;01.08.2024;Security Council Report;Security Council Report;NGO International;I;Project- and programme grant;Bilateral;Project-type interventions;15220;152;Conflict prevention and resolution, peace and security;20;Civilian peace-building, conflict prevention and resolution;;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;Main;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;Support of a program that will advance the transparency

;;
;QZA-24/0092;IFIT - Global Strategic Partnership for Peace and Reconciliation;QZA-0858;QZA Global unspecified;B;2025;21.08.2025;Actual;1 500 000;0;NOK;2024;2025;INSTITUTE FOR INTEGRATED TRANSITIONS;Grant;420901;Seksjon for fred og forsoning;0151;0151.70;0151.70.111;Fred og forsoning, kan overføres;Global Unspecified;YBH;Berggrav, Yngvild;;03;;20.06.2024;IFIT - Institute for Integrated Transitions;IFIT - Institute for Integrated Transitions;NGO Other donor countries;II;General funding grant;Bilateral;Project-type interventions;15220;152;Conflict prevention and resolution, peace and security;20;Civilian peace-building, conflict prevention and resolution;;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;Significant;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;The partnership aims to help fragile and conflict-affected states achieve more successful negotiations and transitions out of war, crisis or authoritarianism. Through its operating model, IFIT seeks to move current practice away from fragmented interventions and toward more integrated solutions that are more likely to strengthen peace and reconciliation. IFIT operates along the following work strains: country brain trusts, thematic practice groups, global initiatives, and research and pulibcations. ;;
;QZA-24/0097;PRIO Centre on Gender, Peace and Security 2024 - 2025;QZA-1047;Peace and Reconciliation 2024;B;2025;19.11.2025;Actual;3 111 955;0;NOK;2024;2025;Instit.for fredsforskning PRIO;Grant;420901;Seksjon for fred og forsoning;0151;0151.70;0151.70.111;Fred og forsoning, kan overføres;Global Unspecified;ELSL;Slåttum, Elisabeth;;03;;01.07.2024;PRIO - International Peace Research Institute, Oslo;PRIO - International Peace Research Institute, Oslo;NGO Norwegian;I;Project- and programme grant;Bilateral;Project-type interventions;15220;152;Conflict prevention and resolution, peace and security;20;Civilian peace-building, conflict prevention and resolution;;-;-;-;-;-;-;Main;Significant;-;Main;-;-;-;-;-;-;The Project’s expected to contribute to enhanced conflict resolution, and  increased inclusion of women and gender perspectives. This will be achieved through research, policy analysis and policy making on peace and conflict, to be shared with policy makers and practitioners within the field of peace and security. The research will specifically focus on countries at war and in conflict, with emphasis on the gendered dimensions of conflict and conflict resolution in general, and specific challenges such as women’s participation, the differential impact of war on different societal groups and sexual and gender based violence.;;
;QZA-24/0099;Robust, integrated and sustainable information systems - DHIS2;GLO-4157;University of Oslo, Department of Informatics, HISP Frame agreement;B;2025;07.05.2025;Actual;6 169 337;0;NOK;2024;2025;Universitetet i Oslo;Grant;300522;VEM-HELSE Seksjon for helse;0160;0160.70;0160.70.125;Faglig bistand - helse;Global Unspecified;NORAD-ITN;Nakkim, Ingunn Merete Tysse;;03;;07.06.2024;UiO - Universitetet i Oslo;UiO - Universitetet i Oslo;Norwegian public sector;I;Project- and programme grant;Bilateral;Other technical assistance;12110;121;Health, general;10;Health policy and administrative management;;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;Main;-;Significant;-;-;Significant;-;-;-;Robust, integrated and sustainable information systems (DHIS2) for improving health and well-being (2024-2028) aims to support countries and regions to strengthen their information systems and information architecture to achieve the SDGs in the health sector and beyond. DHIS2 is an open source free of charge health information system iin use in nearly 80 low- and middle-income countries. This program wil continue to maintain and develop DHIS2 as a Digital Public Goods by supporting core operations, strengthening in-country capacity and the HISP network, improve use and practices in the health sector and beyond, and make sure learning and innovation is shared globally. These priorities are integrated in the UiO/HISP strategy for 2023-2025 and will be integrated in the next strategy period 2026-2028. With climate change and more frequent extreme weather events having serious effects on health, DHIS2 is investing in a Climate and Health pilot in 10 LMICs which integrates climate and weather data into the health information systems. This will strengthen early warning and health response in climate related emergencies, and support countries in modelling the longer-term health effects to inform climate adaptation in the health sector.;;
